Modic changes are changes of the bone marrow next to the endplates that accompany disc degeneration:
type 1, oedema and inflammation: dark on T1, bright on T2 (the most associated with pain, may evolve);
type 2, fatty replacement: bright on T1 and T2 (the most common, stable);
type 3, sclerosis: dark on T1 and T2.
Comparing T1 and T2 is the key. Modic 1 must be told apart from spondylodiscitis: in both the marrow is dark on T1, but in infection the endplates are eroded, the disc is bright on T2 and there are often paravertebral collections.
In a spine degenerated at all levels you still need to look for the level that may explain the symptoms (here the L5-S1 herniation).
